//! Go's `syscall` package for linux/amd64 (docs/adr/0004-system-interface-crate.md).
//!
//! The constants ([`consts`]), struct layouts ([`types`]) and the system-call
//! wrappers (`zsyscall`) are generated by `tools/gen_syscall.go` from Go's own
//! `zerrors_linux_amd64.go`, `zsysnum_linux_amd64.go`, `ztypes_linux_amd64.go`
//! and `zsyscall_linux_amd64.go`; `crates/sys/tests/syscall_expected/mod.rs`
//! checks every constant, struct size and field offset and every error and
//! signal message against go/types. The rest is a port of Go's hand-written
//! helpers.
//!
//! # Mapping from Go
//!
//! - Functions are `snake_case` and constants `SCREAMING_CASE`; Go's struct
//!   names (`Stat_t`, `RawSockaddrInet4`) and snake-cased field names are kept
//!   (`Type` becomes `type_`).
//! - `int` is `isize`, `uint` and `uintptr` are `usize`, `string` is `&str`,
//!   `[]byte` is `&[u8]` (`&mut [u8]` where the kernel writes) and pointers
//!   are references, `Option` for the ones Go callers pass as nil.
//! - `(T, error)` is `Result<T, Errno>`. Where Go returns the raw result next
//!   to an error, only the error is returned.
//! - [`Errno`] and [`Signal`] use Go's Linux numbers and messages on every
//!   host, never the host's own.
//!
//! # Platforms
//!
//! On linux/x86_64 every call is a real system call through the C library's
//! `syscall(2)`. The numbers are linux/amd64's, so on any other target the
//! generated wrappers fail with `ENOSYS`, except a portable subset whose
//! argument shapes are the same in the host's C library: `read`, `write`,
//! `close`, `dup`, `dup2`, `pipe2`, `fcntl` (the descriptor and status-flag
//! commands), `fsync`, `ftruncate`, `fchmod`, `fchown`, `fchdir`, `getpgid`,
//! `setpgid`, `setsid`, `sync`, `umask`, `wait4` and the process and user id
//! queries. Failures of those are translated to Linux error numbers.
//! The helpers that need no kernel (environment, directory-entry parsing,
//! control-message and netlink encoding, [`Sockaddr`] encoding, time
//! conversions) and `fork_exec`/`start_process`/`exec` work everywhere
//! supported; the Linux-only `SysProcAttr` fields need linux/x86_64.
//!
//! The raw entry points ([`syscall`], [`syscall6`], [`raw_syscall`],
//! [`raw_syscall6`], [`all_threads_syscall`], [`all_threads_syscall6`]) are
//! unsafe to call, as arbitrary words go to the kernel.
#![allow(non_upper_case_globals, non_camel_case_types, clippy::too_many_arguments)]

